File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 1 Get some text,  Next: Step 2 Turn the mode on,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.1 Step 1. Get some text
=========================

Open any text file in the language you are learning, or copy a paragraph
and run ‘M-x bltzread-read-text’.  The second is the easiest: it opens a
nicely spaced page.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 2 Turn the mode on,  Next: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Prev: Step 1 Get some text,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.2 Step 2. Turn the mode on
============================

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’.  If asked, type the three-letter language code,
for example ‘slv’, and press Enter.

   You should see: after a moment, words are coloured.  Strong yellow
means new.  Words on your skip list have no colour at all.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Next: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Prev: Step 2 Turn the mode on,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.3 Step 3. Rest on a word
==========================

Move the cursor onto a coloured word and wait half a second.  A small
card appears with the word, its level and, if you set up a dictionary,
its meaning.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Next: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Prev: Step 3 Rest on a word,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.4 Step 4. Mark what you know
==============================

With the cursor on a word, press ‘5’ if you know it perfectly.  The
colour disappears.  Press ‘1’ to make it new again.  Press ‘3’ to say
"familiar".

   Press ‘}’ to jump to the next word you do not know yet.  Press ‘{’ to
go back.  (‘s n’ and ‘s p’ do the same.)  Put a count first to jump
further: ‘C-5 }’ jumps five words (‘M-5 }’ in a terminal).

   A level covers every form of the word, so ‘3’ on _hiše_ also sets
_hiša_.  Made a mistake?  Press ‘u’, or the ordinary undo key, as often
as you like; redo works too.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Next: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Prev: Step 4 Mark what you know,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.5 Step 5. Ignore a name
=========================

Put the cursor on a name and press ‘i’.  It turns grey and bltzread will
stop counting it.  Press ‘i’ again to bring it back.  If a card is in
your way, press ‘c’ to close it; ‘d’ shows the next dictionary entry
when there are several.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Next: Step 7 Leave a note,  Prev: Step 5 Ignore a name,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.6 Step 6. Look a word up properly
===================================

Press ‘l’ on a word, or double-click it.  A longer entry appears.
Looking a word up tells bltzread you did not know it: it restarts that
word's progress.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 7 Leave a note,  Next: Step 8 Turn it off,  Prev: Step 6 Look a word up properly,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.7 Step 7. Leave a note
========================

Press ‘N’, type your own hint, press Enter.  Next time the card shows
it.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 8 Turn it off,  Next: Jump to unknown words with avy,  Prev: Step 7 Leave a note,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.8 Step 8. Turn it off
=======================

Run ‘M-x bltzread-mode’ again.  The colours go and the buffer is
editable.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Jump to unknown words with avy,  Prev: Step 8 Turn it off,  Up: Part 1 Your first page

2.9 Jump to unknown words with avy
==================================

If you have the ‘avy’ package, run ‘M-x bltzread-avy-unknown’.  Every
unknown word on screen gets a hint; type it to go to that word and open
its card.  Escape or ‘C-g’ removes the hints and continues as
‘avy-goto-char-timer’.  Bind it to a key you like.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading,  Next: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date,  Prev: Part 1 Your first page,  Up: Top

3 Part 2: Letting bltzread follow your reading
**********************************************

Now the useful part: bltzread can raise words by itself as you read.

* Menu:

* Step 1. Read: Step 1 Read.
* Step 2. Finish the session: Step 2 Finish the session.
* Step 3. Move your bookmark: Step 3 Move your bookmark.
* Step 4. If you read everything easily: Step 4 If you read everything easily.
* Step 5. Closing the buffer: Step 5 Closing the buffer.
* A warning about books::


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 1 Read,  Next: Step 2 Finish the session,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.1 Step 1. Read
================

Turn the mode on and read a few paragraphs.  Look up the words you do
not know.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 2 Finish the session,  Next: Step 3 Move your bookmark,  Prev: Step 1 Read,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.2 Step 2. Finish the session
==============================

Press ‘s f’.  bltzread asks which part you read.  By default it proposes
everything from the start to the farthest place on screen you reached.
Press Enter to accept.

   You should see a message saying how many words were counted.  Words
you did not look up now have one clean read.  Do this on a few more days
and they climb a level, when they have enough reads.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 3 Move your bookmark,  Next: Step 4 If you read everything easily,  Prev: Step 2 Finish the session,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.3 Step 3. Move your bookmark
==============================

After counting, bltzread may ask whether to move the bookmark to where
you stopped.  Say yes to pick up there next time.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 4 If you read everything easily,  Next: Step 5 Closing the buffer,  Prev: Step 3 Move your bookmark,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.4 Step 4. If you read everything easily
=========================================

Press ‘C-u s f’ to count the range and mark all words in it as known.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 5 Closing the buffer,  Next: A warning about books,  Prev: Step 4 If you read everything easily,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.5 Step 5. Closing the buffer
==============================

If you kill the buffer without finishing, bltzread asks whether to
record your reading.  Answer yes and it takes you through the same
steps.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: A warning about books,  Prev: Step 5 Closing the buffer,  Up: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading

3.6 A warning about books
=========================

In a book opened with nov.el, going to the next chapter replaces the
text.  Finish the session first.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date,  Next: Part 4 Basic configuration,  Prev: Part 2 Letting bltzread follow your reading,  Up: Top

4 Part 3: Keeping bltzr up to date
**********************************

When a word reaches level 5, bltzread queues it instead of changing your
skip list straight away.

* Menu:

* Step 1. Open the review list: Step 1 Open the review list.
* Step 2. Decide: Step 2 Decide.
* Step 3. Apply: Step 3 Apply.
* Step 4. Check: Step 4 Check.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 1 Open the review list,  Next: Step 2 Decide,  Up: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date

4.1 Step 1. Open the review list
================================

Run ‘M-x bltzread-review’.  You see words and their basic forms.  All
are marked "add".


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 2 Decide,  Next: Step 3 Apply,  Prev: Step 1 Open the review list,  Up: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date

4.2 Step 2. Decide
==================

Move down with ‘n’ and ‘p’.  Press ‘d’ for words you want to think about
later and ‘r’ to say "not really known": those drop back to level 4.
Press ‘A’ to mark all as add again.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 3 Apply,  Next: Step 4 Check,  Prev: Step 2 Decide,  Up: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date

4.3 Step 3. Apply
=================

Press ‘x’.  Confirm.  The words are added to your skip list; a ‘.bak’
copy of the old list is kept beside it.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 4 Check,  Prev: Step 3 Apply,  Up: Part 3 Keeping bltzr up to date

4.4 Step 4. Check
=================

Run bltzr on your text.  The words you added no longer appear as new.


