8 Part 7: Reading along with subtle
***********************************

If you play audio or video with subtle, bltzread can credit only what
was played.  Put this in your init file:
     (setq bltzread-range-farthest-source #'subtle-played-through-position
           bltzread-range-start-function  #'subtle-played-start-position)

     (add-hook 'subtle-buffer-setup-hook
               (lambda ()
                 (when (= subtle-track-index 0)
                   (setq bltzread-language subtle-language)
                   (bltzread-mode 1))))

     (add-hook 'subtle-pause-hook
               (lambda () (when bltzread-mode (bltzread-avy-unknown))))
   Now bltzread is on for the first subtitle file only.  Press ‘p’ to
pause and the unknown words in view get avy hints; pick one to see its
card.  In a subtitle buffer the same single keys work (‘1’-‘5’, ‘l’,
‘}’, ‘{’); subtle keeps ‘p f b F B ] [ = ) ( RET r h ? q’ and the prefix
‘s’.  The first load of a long transcript takes a moment while bltzr
reads all its words.  See the reference manual for the details.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Short dictionary entries,  Next: If something goes wrong,  Prev: Part 7 Reading along with subtle,  Up: Top

9 Short dictionary entries
**************************

The card that appears when you rest on a word shows only the part of a
dictionary entry you care about, for example the English translations.
The lookup key shows the whole entry.  bltzread knows nothing about any
particular dictionary: you tell it, per dictionary, which pieces of the
entry to show.  This takes a line or two each.

* Menu:

* Step 1. Look at the raw entry: Step 1 Look at the raw entry.
* Step 2. Write a spec: Step 2 Write a spec.
* Step 3. My five dictionaries: Step 3 My five dictionaries.
* Step 4. Test it: Step 4 Test it.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 1 Look at the raw entry,  Next: Step 2 Write a spec,  Up: Short dictionary entries

9.1 Step 1. Look at the raw entry
=================================

An entry is HTML.  Print it with sdcv (‘-u’ takes the dictionary name
from ‘sdcv -l -2 ~/archive/_goldendict’):
     sdcv -n -j -e -0 -1 -2 ~/archive/_goldendict -u "Polish-English" dom
   The output is JSON; the entry is in the field ‘definition’.  Read it
for the pieces you want.  In the Polish-English entry the translations
look like this:
     <span class="trg"><span class="trans">building </span></span>
   In the Slovene one the translations are all in one paragraph,
separated by middle dots:
     <p class="gloss">house · household, family</p>
   In the Pali one the meaning is the first bold text:
     <div class=dpd><p>masc. <b>nature; character</b>


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 2 Write a spec,  Next: Step 3 My five dictionaries,  Prev: Step 1 Look at the raw entry,  Up: Short dictionary entries

9.2 Step 2. Write a spec
========================

In ‘bltzread-dict-filters’ each dictionary gets a spec.  The key is the
dictionary name or the language code.  A spec is one of:

   • A regexp string.  Every match in the HTML gives one line: the text
     of its first group (or the whole match).  For the Polish entry,
     this regexp means "the text right after class="trans">":
          "class=\"trans\">\\([^<]*\\)"
   • ‘(:class "gloss")’: the text just after each element with that
     class.  Shorter than a regexp when the class is all you need.  Give
     a list for several classes.
   • Extra keys for a list spec: ‘:split " · "’ cuts each piece into
     separate lines, ‘:max 5’ keeps the first five lines, ‘:join ", "’
     puts the lines on one line.
   • A function taking the HTML, for anything else.

   Tags and entities are removed, repeated lines dropped.  Do not
include the headword or the dictionary name: the card shows the word
already.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 3 My five dictionaries,  Next: Step 4 Test it,  Prev: Step 2 Write a spec,  Up: Short dictionary entries

9.3 Step 3. My five dictionaries
================================

     (setq bltzread-dict-filters
           '(("slv" :class "gloss" :split " · ")               ; DSSJ-E-sl-en
             ("pli" . "<div class=dpd><p>[^<]*<b>\\([^<]*\\)") ; dpd
             ("pol" . "class=\"trans\">\\([^<]*\\)")         ; Polish-English
             ("hrv" . "class=\"trans\">\\([^<]*\\)")         ; Croatian-English
             ("eng" :class "df" :max 5)))                      ; new-oxford-american-dictionary
   Remember that inside a Lisp string every backslash is doubled.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: Step 4 Test it,  Prev: Step 3 My five dictionaries,  Up: Short dictionary entries

9.4 Step 4. Test it
===================

Evaluate the ‘setq’, then rest on a word.  If the card still shows the
whole entry, your spec found nothing (bltzread then falls back to the
full entry on purpose).  Try the spec on its own with ‘M-x
eval-expression’:
     (bltzread-dict-apply-filter '(:class "gloss" :split " · ")
       "<p class=\"gloss\">house · family</p>")
   This returns ‘"house\nfamily"’.  Lookups are cached with the entry;
run ‘M-x bltzread-dict-clear-cache’ after changing a spec.


File: bltzread-tutorial.info,  Node: If something goes wrong,  Prev: Short dictionary entries,  Up: Top

10 If something goes wrong
**************************

No colours
     Check ‘bltzr list-languages’ and that the language code is right.
No dictionary text
     Run ‘sdcv -l -2 YOUR-FOLDER’ in a terminal and compare names with
     ‘bltzread-sdcv-dictionaries’.
